markdown <<MARKDOWN
# How Does an App Get Live Data? Intro to APIs

<img src="https://images.code.org/ec00814b3780812d00fe6876a97daedc-image-1770743841734.png" alt="partly cloudy weather icon" width="250px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 40px;">

#### Many apps show information that doesn’t come from their own code.
#### Weather apps, maps, music apps, and sports apps often pull data from somewhere else.
#### So how does an app “ask” another service for information?

#### In this level, you’ll learn what an API is and how it helps apps communicate with other systems.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

---

#### **Think about:**

- #### What might an app need to “ask for” instead of storing itself?
- #### Why might an app use another service instead of building everything on its own?

#### 👉 **What kinds of apps do you use that rely on live, changing information?**

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em; font-weight: bold;">💡 Check your thinking:</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 20px; margin-left: 20px; margin-right: 20px; margin-bottom:75px;">
    <p style="font-size: 14pt;">
Many apps don’t store all the information they show. Instead, they pull live data from another service, like weather forecasts, maps, or movie times. That means the app has to communicate with another system to request the data and receive it back.
</p>
</div>
</details>


<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">
📖 An <strong>API (Application Programming Interface)</strong> is a set of rules that allows software applications to communicate with each other.
</p>
</div>

---

##  “Asking for Data”

<img src="https://images.code.org/5f49c06b5874c513ec0055162e10b53c-image-1770743675458.png" alt="simple diagram showing client app sending request to server and receiving response" width="650px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

---


## What Happens When an App Uses an API?

#### When an app uses an API, it’s usually doing something like this:

- #### The **client app** sends a **request** asking for data  
- #### A **server** receives the request  
- #### The server sends back a **response** with the data  

#### This is how apps can show **live, up-to-date information** that changes over time.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em; font-weight: bold;">💡 Check your thinking:</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 20px; margin-left: 20px; margin-right: 20px; margin-bottom:75px;">
    <p style="font-size: 14pt;">
The app doesn’t “grab” data magically—it follows a predictable pattern: a <strong>request</strong> goes out, and a <strong>response</strong> comes back. The app is the <strong>client</strong>, and the service providing the data is the <strong>server</strong>.
</p>
</div>
</details>

---

<div style="background-color: #dccbea; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%; margin-bottom: 8px;">
<strong>🧠 Terms to know:</strong>
</p>
<p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 135%; margin: 0;">
<strong>Client:</strong> The app asking for data<br>
<strong>Server:</strong> The service that stores and sends data<br>
<strong>Request:</strong> A message asking for data<br>
<strong>Response:</strong> A message that sends data back
</p>
</div>

---

## Next up

#### Next, you’ll look at what kind of data APIs often return (like JSON) and why using APIs means your app becomes part of a larger system.

MARKDOWN
